          "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/flaviendelangle/events{/privacy}",
          "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/flaviendelangle/received_events",
          "type": "User",
          "user_view_type": "public",
          "site_admin": false
        },
        "created_at": "2025-01-01T15:17:54Z",
        "updated_at": "2025-01-01T15:17:54Z",
        "author_association": "MEMBER",
        "body": "> The React context is hosted in a separate package than the component. So people needs to install multiple versions of the license package, which seems hard to figure out which version to install.\r\n\r\nOr we would need to have all the package the person use be compatible with a shared version of the license package.\r\n\r\nI have a side question that I think is related: how do we want to version the license package once we have a MUI X package (that will be in v9, v10 etc...) and a Base UI X package (that will be in v1, v2, etc... if I understand correctly).\r\nOr do we plan to have 2 license packages? One for MUI X and one for Base UI X?\r\n\r\nIf we need to decouple the version of the license package with the version of the commercial product packages, then maybe we should just avoid BCs at all cost on it.\r\nWe would have a `@new-component-name/license` package that would be in v1 for several years, and the day we absolutely need to update to a v2, we would make sure this v2 is still compatible with as many previous major versions as possible to make sure everybody can always use a single version of the license package.\r\n\r\nNot saying that it's ideal of course :grimacing: ",
